This is the perfect way to master the "Security Guard" logic of the Builder Pattern. By making Milk compulsory and the others optional, you are creating a "Contract" that the user must follow.
Here is the complete, production-grade code with a detailed breakdown of how it works.
1. The Complete Code: Mandatory Milk Edition
public class Tea {
    // These are final - once built, they never change! (Immutability)
    private final String milkType;  // COMPULSORY
    private final boolean hasSugar; // OPTIONAL
    private final boolean hasGinger;// OPTIONAL

    // 1. Private Constructor: Only the Builder can call this
    private Tea(Builder builder) {
        this.milkType = builder.milkType;
        this.hasSugar = builder.hasSugar;
        this.hasGinger = builder.hasGinger;
    }

    // 2. The Static Inner Class (The Builder)
    public static class Builder {
        private String milkType;    // No default, starts as null
        private boolean hasSugar = false;  // Default: No sugar
        private boolean hasGinger = false; // Default: No ginger

        // This method sets the COMPULSORY field
        public Builder setMilkType(String milkType) {
            this.milkType = milkType;
            return this; // Method Chaining
        }

        public Builder addSugar() {
            this.hasSugar = true;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder addGinger() {
            this.hasGinger = true;
            return this;
        }

        // 3. The "Security Guard" Method
        public Tea build() {
            // Validation Logic
            if (this.milkType == null || this.milkType.isEmpty()) {
                // This is the Run-time error if you forget Milk!
                throw new IllegalStateException("Order Failed: You MUST specify a milk type (Dairy/Oat/Soy)!");
            }
            
            // If the guard lets you pass, the Tea is born
            return new Tea(this);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Your Tea: [Milk=" + milkType + ", Sugar=" + hasSugar + ", Ginger=" + hasGinger + "]";
    }
}

2. How it Behaves (The "Success" vs. "Fail" Test)
✅ The Success Scenario
Since Milk is provided, the builder works perfectly.
Tea myTea = new Tea.Builder()
                .setMilkType("Dairy") // Compulsory field provided
                .addSugar()           // Optional
                .build();

System.out.println(myTea); 
// Output: Your Tea: [Milk=Dairy, Sugar=true, Ginger=false]

❌ The Failure Scenario
If you forget to call .setMilkType(), the code will compile, but when you run it:
Tea brokenTea = new Tea.Builder()
                   .addGinger()
                   .build(); // <--- CRASH! IllegalStateException thrown here.

3. Detailed Step-by-Step Breakdown
I. The "Default Value" Strategy
Notice in the Builder class, I set hasSugar = false.
 * Why? Since these are optional, we give them a safe "starting value." If the user doesn't call .addSugar(), the tea just comes without sugar.
II. The "Null" Trap
For milkType, I did not set a default. It starts as null.
 * The Problem: If we don't call .setMilkType(), the milkType stays null.
 * The Solution: The if (this.milkType == null) check in the build() method stops the process before a "null milk" tea can ever reach your system.
III. Why is this better than a Constructor?
If you had a constructor like new Tea("Dairy", true, false), and you accidentally swapped the parameters or left one out, the code might still run but create a "weird" tea. With the Builder, the names of the methods (setMilkType, addSugar) make it impossible to get confused.
🛠️ Summary for your "GOAT" Journey
| Part | Role in this Example |
|---|---|
| return this | Acts as the "Bridge" so you can add Ginger after Milk in one line. |
| .build() | Acts as the "Security Guard" checking if you provided the Milk. |
| IllegalStateException | The "Alarm Bell" that rings if you try to build an inconsistent object. |
